e and after restart (cmd-c54e455aa93e4206, cmd-f0803c11669c43e0).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3e3328f2c16f4253\",\"cmd-653d572f02374d24\",\"cmd-0b6c6076810d4be5\",\"cmd-c54e455aa93e4206\",\"cmd-f0803c11669c43e0\"]},{\"id\":\"seven-compressed-rotations\",\"requirement\":\"Keep seven compressed rotations\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Final config uses rotate 7, compress, delaycompress. Nine forced rotation cycles against the final config (cmd-b56485023bde4464) produced exactly seven retained rotated files (app.log.1 uncompressed per delaycompress semantics, app.log.2.gz through app.log.7.gz compressed), with older rotations pruned, directly demonstrating both the retention cap and compression.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-653d572f02374d24\",\"cmd-b56485023bde4464\"]},{\"id\":\"continue-writing-after-rotation\",\"requirement\":\"Allow the application to continue writing after rotation\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A live writer process (PID 1569) appended continuously to app.log; a forced rotation truncated app.log via copytruncate while the writer kept running uninterrupted, prior content was preserved in the rotated file, and subsequent writes correctly landed in the fresh app.log (cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c, cmd-4a66f220c66b4d92, cmd-5f92bb3071de41fe).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c\",\"cmd-4a66f220c66b4d92\",\"cmd-5f92bb3071de41fe\"]},{\"id\":\"provider-wide-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1 (independent of the public task contract)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart baseline captured boot_id and healthy config/timer/log state (cmd-6a908a8f150d4109), a reboot was triggered and acknowledged before disconnect (cmd-7d4417489f954ab8), connectivity loss then recovery was observed (cmd-4606fbc994c24a57, cmd-72ebc5c7be3a432f), and post-restart state shows a different boot_id together with recovered logrotate config, active timer, and intact app.log (cmd-f0803c11669c43e0). This is corroborated by the fixed global boot-identity snapshots (before_prepare/after_prepare = 513c865e..., after_executor = 11ea8768...).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6a908a8f150d4109\",\"cmd-7d4417489f954ab8\",\"cmd-4606fbc994c24a57\",\"cmd-72ebc5c7be3a432f\",\"cmd-f0803c11669c43e0\",\"global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"The executor performed several bounded, low-risk experimental mutations while validating the config, and reverted nearly all of them. An initial config with 'dateext' (cmd-3e3328f2c16f4253) caused same-day forced-rotation filename collisions during an 8-cycle retention test (cmd-f1a0f7db1d784abd); the resulting stray file was removed and the config rewritten without dateext (cmd-653d572f02374d24), a valid corrective action. A background test writer process and its script/pid/log files (cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c) and rotation test artifacts from a 9-cycle retention run (cmd-b56485023bde4464) were fully cleaned up and app.log restored to its original prepared content (cmd-3c169ce198e04bc9), confirmed by the after_prepare vs after_executor temporary-files diff showing no residual logrotate test files. One minor residue remains: /tmp/reboot.log (created by the backgrounded reboot command cmd-7d4417489f954ab8) was left in /tmp and is visible in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot; it is small, non-sensitive, and a direct byproduct of the required restart protocol rather than an unrelated experiment. No failed services, unsafe exposure, or package-state damage were introduced; listening-sockets and failed-services snapshots are unchanged apart from expected reboot-driven PID changes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f1a0f7db1d784abd\",\"cmd-653d572f02374d24\",\"cmd-3c169ce198e04bc9\",\"cmd-7d4417489f954ab8\",\"global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_prepare:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, empirical evidence: daily scheduling via logrotate.d config plus systemd timer, a proven seven-rotation compressed retention cap demonstrated across nine forced cycles, and continuous application writing across a rotation via copytruncate with a live writer process. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed cleanly, with a genuine boot-identity change and full post-restart recovery of the rotation configuration, timer, and log file. Hygiene is good: an early misconfiguration (dateext) and several test mutations were self-corrected and cleaned up, restoring app.log to its original prepared content, with only a trivial /tmp/reboot.log artifact left behind.\",\"limitations\":[\"No evidence of an actual midnight logrotate.timer firing occurred within the observation window; daily cadence is inferred from timer configuration and forced-rotation behavior rather than an unattended scheduled trigger.\",\"The final live-writer copytruncate demonstration (cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c/cmd-4a66f220c66b4d92/cmd-5f92bb3071de41fe) was performed against an earlier config revision (with dateext) rather than re-run against the final config used for the retention test; behavior is expected to be identical since copytruncate/daily/rotate were unchanged, but this was not independently re-verified against the exact final config file.\",\"/tmp/reboot.log was left on node1 after the restart protocol; this is minor residue not addressed by any cleanup command.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"daily-rotation","requirement":"Rotate /var/log/inventory/app.log daily","status":"satisfied","summary":"logrotate.d config written with 'daily' directive (cmd-3e3328f2c16f4253, final version in cmd-653d572f02374d24), syntax validated via logrotate -d (cmd-0b6c6076810d4be5), and logrotate.timer confirmed enabled/active with a scheduled daily midnight trigger both before and after restart (cmd-c54e455aa93e4206, cmd-f0803c11669c43e0).","evidence_ids":["cmd-3e3328f2c16f4253","cmd-653d572f02374d24","cmd-0b6c6076810d4be5","cmd-c54e455aa93e4206","cmd-f0803c11669c43e0"]},{"id":"seven-compressed-rotations","requirement":"Keep seven compressed rotations","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final config uses rotate 7, compress, delaycompress. Nine forced rotation cycles against the final config (cmd-b56485023bde4464) produced exactly seven retained rotated files (app.log.1 uncompressed per delaycompress semantics, app.log.2.gz through app.log.7.gz compressed), with older rotations pruned, directly demonstrating both the retention cap and compression.","evidence_ids":["cmd-653d572f02374d24","cmd-b56485023bde4464"]},{"id":"continue-writing-after-rotation","requirement":"Allow the application to continue writing after rotation","status":"satisfied","summary":"A live writer process (PID 1569) appended continuously to app.log; a forced rotation truncated app.log via copytruncate while the writer kept running uninterrupted, prior content was preserved in the rotated file, and subsequent writes correctly landed in the fresh app.log (cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c, cmd-4a66f220c66b4d92, cmd-5f92bb3071de41fe).","evidence_ids":["cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c","cmd-4a66f220c66b4d92","cmd-5f92bb3071de41fe"]},{"id":"provider-wide-restart-protocol","requirement":"Node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1 (independent of the public task contract)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart baseline captured boot_id and healthy config/timer/log state (cmd-6a908a8f150d4109), a reboot was triggered and acknowledged before disconnect (cmd-7d4417489f954ab8), connectivity loss then recovery was observed (cmd-4606fbc994c24a57, cmd-72ebc5c7be3a432f), and post-restart state shows a different boot_id together with recovered logrotate config, active timer, and intact app.log (cmd-f0803c11669c43e0). This is corroborated by the fixed global boot-identity snapshots (before_prepare/after_prepare = 513c865e..., after_executor = 11ea8768...).","evidence_ids":["cmd-6a908a8f150d4109","cmd-7d4417489f954ab8","cmd-4606fbc994c24a57","cmd-72ebc5c7be3a432f","cmd-f0803c11669c43e0","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"The executor performed several bounded, low-risk experimental mutations while validating the config, and reverted nearly all of them. An initial config with 'dateext' (cmd-3e3328f2c16f4253) caused same-day forced-rotation filename collisions during an 8-cycle retention test (cmd-f1a0f7db1d784abd); the resulting stray file was removed and the config rewritten without dateext (cmd-653d572f02374d24), a valid corrective action. A background test writer process and its script/pid/log files (cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c) and rotation test artifacts from a 9-cycle retention run (cmd-b56485023bde4464) were fully cleaned up and app.log restored to its original prepared content (cmd-3c169ce198e04bc9), confirmed by the after_prepare vs after_executor temporary-files diff showing no residual logrotate test files. One minor residue remains: /tmp/reboot.log (created by the backgrounded reboot command cmd-7d4417489f954ab8) was left in /tmp and is visible in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot; it is small, non-sensitive, and a direct byproduct of the required restart protocol rather than an unrelated experiment. No failed services, unsafe exposure, or package-state damage were introduced; listening-sockets and failed-services snapshots are unchanged apart from expected reboot-driven PID changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f1a0f7db1d784abd","cmd-653d572f02374d24","cmd-3c169ce198e04bc9","cmd-7d4417489f954ab8","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All three material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, empirical evidence: daily scheduling via logrotate.d config plus systemd timer, a proven seven-rotation compressed retention cap demonstrated across nine forced cycles, and continuous application writing across a rotation via copytruncate with a live writer process. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed cleanly, with a genuine boot-identity change and full post-restart recovery of the rotation configuration, timer, and log file. Hygiene is good: an early misconfiguration (dateext) and several test mutations were self-corrected and cleaned up, restoring app.log to its original prepared content, with only a trivial /tmp/reboot.log artifact left behind.","limitations":["No evidence of an actual midnight logrotate.timer firing occurred within the observation window; daily cadence is inferred from timer configuration and forced-rotation behavior rather than an unattended scheduled trigger.","The final live-writer copytruncate demonstration (cmd-b85a366a6b9a4f1c/cmd-4a66f220c66b4d92/cmd-5f92bb3071de41fe) was performed against an earlier config revision (with dateext) rather than re-run against the final config used for the retention test; behavior is expected to be identical since copytruncate/daily/rotate were unchanged, but this was not independently re-verified against the exact final config file.","/tmp/reboot.log was left on node1 after the restart protocol; this is minor residue not addressed by any cleanup command."]},"ttft_ms":21536,"type":"result","duration_ms":66664,"uuid":"2b6fb45b-2ad5-449e-ac01-4de36300478b","ttft_stream_ms":896,"time_to_request_ms":226,"first_content_frame_ms":896,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
